Q8 — PERFORM UNIT TESTING USING JUNIT
AIM
Create a Maven Calculator project, write arithmetic methods, create JUnit test cases, run tests, inspect
test reports, demonstrate a failed test and then correct it.
PART A — CHECK JAVA AND MAVEN
1. Open Command Prompt or Git Bash.
Explanation: You need a terminal to check the development tools and run Maven commands.
2. Check the Java runtime:
java -version
Explanation: Displays the installed Java runtime version.
3. Check the Java compiler:
javac -version
Explanation: Confirms that a JDK compiler is installed. Maven Java projects need a JDK.
4. Check Maven:
mvn -version
Explanation: Displays Maven's version and the Java version Maven is using. If 'mvn' is not recognised,
Maven is not installed or its bin folder is not on PATH.
PART B — CREATE THE MAVEN PROJECT FOLDERS
5. Move to the Desktop:
cd ~/Desktop
Explanation: Creates the practical project in an easy-to-find location. In Windows Command Prompt, use cd
%USERPROFILE%\Desktop instead.
6. Create the project folder:
mkdir JUnitCalculator
Explanation: Creates the root folder for the Maven project.
7. Enter the folder:
cd JUnitCalculator
Explanation: All following project files and Maven commands will be created/run here.
8. Create the Maven source and test folders:
mkdir -p src/main/java
mkdir -p src/test/java
Explanation: Maven conventionally stores application code in src/main/java and test code in src/test/java. In
Windows Command Prompt, create these folders using File Explorer or mkdir commands one folder at a
time.
PART C — CREATE pom.xml
9. Create a file named pom.xml in the JUnitCalculator root folder.
Explanation: pom.xml is Maven's Project Object Model file. It defines the project identity, Java compiler
settings and external libraries.
10. Put this content in pom.xml and save it:
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0
https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
<modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
<groupId>com.example</groupId>
<artifactId>junit-calculator</artifactId>
<version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
<properties>
<maven.compiler.source>8</maven.compiler.source>
<maven.compiler.target>8</maven.compiler.target>
<project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
</properties>
<dependencies>
<dependency>
<groupId>junit</groupId>
<artifactId>junit</artifactId>
<version>4.13.2</version>
<scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
</dependencies>
</project>

PART D — CREATE Calculator.java
11. Inside src/main/java, create Calculator.java.
Explanation: This is the application class containing the arithmetic methods being tested.
12. Enter and save this code:
public class Calculator {
public int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
public int subtract(int a, int b) {
return a - b;
}
public int multiply(int a, int b) {
return a * b;
}
public double divide(int a, int b) {
if (b == 0) {
throw new IllegalArgumentException("Cannot divide by zero");
}
return (double) a / b;
}
}
PART E — CREATE CalculatorTest.java
13. Inside src/test/java, create CalculatorTest.java.
Explanation: Maven's standard test source folder is src/test/java. JUnit test classes are placed here.
14. Enter and save this code:
import org.junit.Test;
import static org.junit.Assert.*;
public class CalculatorTest {
Calculator calculator = new Calculator();
@Test
public void testAddition() {
assertEquals(10, calculator.add(5, 5));
}
@Test
public void testSubtraction() {
assertEquals(5, calculator.subtract(10, 5));
}
@Test
public void testMultiplication() {
assertEquals(25, calculator.multiply(5, 5));
}
@Test
public void testDivision() {
assertEquals(2.0, calculator.divide(10, 5), 0.001);
}
@Test(expected = IllegalArgumentException.class)
public void testDivisionByZero() {
calculator.divide(10, 0);
}
}
Explanation:
- @Test marks a method as a JUnit test.
- assertEquals compares the expected result with the actual result.
- For double values, the third argument is a tolerance (delta).
- expected = IllegalArgumentException verifies that dividing by zero throws the expected exception.
- Each test checks one behaviour of Calculator.
PART F — RUN THE TESTS
15. Return to the JUnitCalculator root folder (the folder containing pom.xml).
Explanation: Maven commands should be run from the project root so Maven can find pom.xml.
16. Run:
mvn clean test
Explanation:
- clean removes files from the previous build under target.
- test compiles the source and test code, then runs the JUnit tests.
- The first run may take longer because Maven downloads plugins and JUnit.
17. Wait for Maven to finish.
Explanation: Do not close the terminal while Maven is downloading dependencies or running tests.
18. Read the summary near the end of the output.
Explanation: Maven reports the number of tests, failures, errors and skipped tests. A successful run ends
with BUILD SUCCESS.
PART G — VIEW THE GENERATED TEST REPORT
19. Open the target/surefire-reports folder.
Explanation: Maven Surefire generates test result reports in this folder after running tests.
20. Find files similar to:
TEST-CalculatorTest.xml
CalculatorTest.txt
Explanation: The XML report contains structured test results; the text report provides a readable summary.
21. Open CalculatorTest.txt.
Explanation: Confirm the test count and that failures/errors are zero for the corrected test suite.
PART H — DEMONSTRATE A FAILED TEST AND CORRECT IT
22. Open CalculatorTest.java.
Explanation: You will deliberately make one expected result incorrect to demonstrate that JUnit detects a
failed assertion.
23. Temporarily change the addition assertion to:
assertEquals(11, calculator.add(5, 5));
Explanation: The expected value is intentionally wrong; 5 + 5 is 10, not 11.
24. Save the file and run:
mvn test
Explanation: Maven reruns the tests. The addition test should fail because expected 11 does not match
actual 10.
25. Read the failure summary.
Explanation: It should identify the failing test and show the expected and actual values.
26. Correct the assertion back to:
assertEquals(10, calculator.add(5, 5));
Explanation: The expected value is now correct.
27. Save and run:
mvn clean test
Explanation: This performs a clean test run after correcting the test.
28. Verify BUILD SUCCESS and zero failures/errors.
Explanation: This demonstrates both detection of an incorrect test expectation and successful execution
after correction.